6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B1, B2} 

Colleagues 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 have the core liberty count right: the isolated C1-C2 black group begins with exactly two liberties at B1 and B2. White B1 fills one; B2 then becomes suicide for Black because every adjacent intersection is occupied by Black stones or the new White stone, producing no capture that would reopen the point. White B2 places the same group in atari; Black’s only legal reply is B1 (A1 still supplies a temporary liberty), after which A1 itself becomes the final liberty that White occupies on the next turn. Both sequences therefore force capture regardless of Black’s replies elsewhere. 

Colleague 4’s objection that Black can answer B1 with B2 to link toward B3-B4 and create a false eye at A1 does not hold under the actual surrounding stones: after White B1 the point B2 remains fully surrounded, so the linking move itself is illegal suicide and cannot restore liberties or connect the groups. The B3-B4 chain already has its own separate liberties at A3/A4 and C5, but that does not change the fact that C1-C2 is captured first. No other first move (A3, C5, D3, etc.) reduces any Black unit to a single forced liberty against every possible reply.

